NNAAMMEE
     ffssddbb — FFS debugging/editing tool

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ffssddbb [--dd] [--ff] [--rr] _f_s_n_a_m_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ffssddbb utility opens _f_s_n_a_m_e (usually a raw disk partition) and runs a
     command loop allowing manipulation of the file system's inode data.  You
     are prompted to enter a command with ffssddbb ((iinnuumm XX))>> where _X is the cur‐
     rently selected i-number.  The initial selected inode is the root of the
     file system (i-number 2).  The command processor uses the editline(3) li‐
     brary, so you can use command line editing to reduce typing if desired.
     When you exit the command loop, the file system superblock is marked
     dirty and any buffered blocks are written to the file system.

     The following options are available:

     --dd      Enable additional debugging output (which comes primarily from
             fsck(8)-derived code).

     --ff      Left for historical reasons and has no meaning.

     --rr      Open the file system read/only, and disables all commands that
             would write to it.

CCOOMMMMAANNDDSS
     Besides the built-in editline(3) commands, ffssddbb supports these commands:

     hheellpp    Print out the list of accepted commands.

     iinnooddee _i_-_n_u_m_b_e_r
             Select inode _i_-_n_u_m_b_e_r as the new current inode.

     bbaacckk    Revert to the previously current inode.

     ccllrrii _i_-_n_u_m_b_e_r
             Clear _i_-_n_u_m_b_e_r.

     llooookkuupp _n_a_m_e
     ccdd _n_a_m_e
             Find _n_a_m_e in the current directory and make its inode the current
             inode.  _N_a_m_e may be a multi-component name or may begin with
             slash to indicate that the root inode should be used to start the
             lookup.  If some component along the pathname is not found, the
             last valid directory encountered is left as the active inode.
             This command is valid only if the starting inode is a directory.

     aaccttiivvee
     pprriinntt   Print out the active inode.

     bblloocckkss  Print out the block list of the active inode.  Note that the
             printout can become long for large files, since all indirect
             block pointers will also be printed.

     ffiinnddbbllkk _d_i_s_k___b_l_o_c_k___n_u_m_b_e_r _._._.
             Find the inode(s) owning the specified disk block(s) number(s).
             Note that these are not absolute disk blocks numbers, but offsets
             from the start of the partition.

     uupplliinnkk  Increment the active inode's link count.

     ddoowwnnlliinnkk
             Decrement the active inode's link count.

     lliinnkkccoouunntt _n_u_m_b_e_r
             Set the active inode's link count to _n_u_m_b_e_r.

     llss      List the current inode's directory entries.  This command is
             valid only if the current inode is a directory.

     rrmm _n_a_m_e
     ddeell _n_a_m_e
             Remove the entry _n_a_m_e from the current directory inode.  This
             command is valid only if the current inode is a directory.

     llnn _i_n_o _n_a_m_e
             Create a link to inode _i_n_o under the name _n_a_m_e in the current di‐
             rectory inode.  This command is valid only if the current inode
             is a directory.

     cchhiinnuumm _d_i_r_s_l_o_t _i_n_u_m
             Change the i-number in directory entry _d_i_r_s_l_o_t to _i_n_u_m.

     cchhnnaammee _d_i_r_s_l_o_t _n_a_m_e
             Change the name in directory entry _d_i_r_s_l_o_t to _n_a_m_e.  This command
             cannot expand a directory entry.  You can only rename an entry if
             the name will fit into the existing directory slot.

     cchhttyyppee _t_y_p_e
             Change the type of the current inode to _t_y_p_e.  _T_y_p_e may be one
             of: _f_i_l_e, _d_i_r, _s_o_c_k_e_t, or _f_i_f_o.

     cchhmmoodd _m_o_d_e
             Change the mode bits of the current inode to _m_o_d_e.  You cannot
             change the file type with this subcommand; use cchhttyyppee to do that.

     cchhffllaaggss _f_l_a_g_s
             Change the file flags of the current inode to _f_l_a_g_s.

     cchhoowwnn _u_i_d
             Change the owner of the current inode to _u_i_d.

     cchhggrrpp _g_i_d
             Change the group of the current inode to _g_i_d.

     cchhggeenn _g_e_n
             Change the generation number of the current inode to _g_e_n.

     bbttiimmee _t_i_m_e
     mmttiimmee _t_i_m_e
     ccttiimmee _t_i_m_e
     aattiimmee _t_i_m_e
             Change the creation (birth), modification, change, or access time
             (respectively) on the current inode to _t_i_m_e.  _T_i_m_e should be in
             the format _Y_Y_Y_Y_M_M_D_D_H_H_M_M_S_S_[_._n_s_e_c_] where _n_s_e_c is an optional
             nanosecond specification.  If no nanoseconds are specified, the
             _b_i_r_t_h_n_s_e_c, _m_t_i_m_e_n_s_e_c, _c_t_i_m_e_n_s_e_c, or _a_t_i_m_e_n_s_e_c field will be set
             to zero.  Note that bbttiimmee is available on UFS2 file systems only.

     qquuiitt, qq, eexxiitt, _<_E_O_F_>
